== 3. Lessening Sin

The third device that Satan has to draw the soul to sin is to extenuate and lessen sin. “Ah,” says Satan, “it is only a little pride, a little worldliness, a little uncleanness, a little drunkenness, etc.” As Lot said of Zoar,#footnote[Gen 19:20.] “it is only a little one, and my soul will live.” “Alas,” says Satan, “it is only a very little sin that you stick at so. You may commit it without any danger to your soul. It is only a little one; you may commit it, and yet your soul will live.”

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are these.]

First, consider solemnly that those sins which we are apt to count small have brought on men the greatest wrath of God: such as the eating of an apple, gathering a few sticks on the Sabbath day, and touching the ark. Oh, the dreadful wrath that these sins brought down on the#footnote[Draco, the rigid lawgiver, being asked why he appointed death for all sins when sins were not equal, answered that he knew sins were not all equal, but he knew the least deserved death. So though the sins of men are not all equal, yet the least of them deserves eternal death.] heads and hearts of men! The least sin is contrary to the law of God, the nature of God, the being of God and the glory of God, and therefore it is often punished severely by God. And do we not see daily the vengeance of the Almighty falling on the bodies, names, estates, families and souls of men for those sins that are only little ones in their eyes? Surely, if we are not utterly forsaken by God and blinded by Satan, we cannot fail to see it. Oh, therefore, when Satan says, “It is only a little one,” you must say, “Oh, but those sins that you call little are such as will cause God to rain hell out of heaven on sinners, as he did on the Sodomites.”

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider seriously that giving way to a lesser sin makes way for committing a greater. If a man yields to a lesser sin to avoid a greater, it is ten thousand to one that God in justice will leave that soul to fall into a greater. If we commit one sin to avoid another, it is just that we should avoid neither. For we have neither law nor power in our own hands to keep off sin as we please, and by yielding to the#footnote[Ps 137:9: “Blessed is he who seizes your little ones and dashes them against the stones.” Hugo’s gloss is devout, etc. (#emph[Sit nihil in te Babylonicum]): let there be nothing of Babylon in you. Not only the grown men but the little ones must be dashed against the stones; not only great sins but little sins must be killed, or they will kill the soul forever.] lesser, we tempt the tempter to tempt us to the greater. Sin is of an encroaching nature. It creeps on the soul by degrees, step by step, until it has brought the soul to the very height of sin. David gives way to his wandering eye, and this led him to those foul sins that caused God to break his bones, to turn his day into night, and to leave his soul in great darkness. Jacob and Peter and other saints have found this true by woeful experience: yielding to a lesser sin has been the ushering in of a greater. The little thief will open the door and make way for the greater, and the little wedge knocked in will make way for the greater. Satan will first draw you to sit with the drunkard, then to sip with the drunkard, and then at last to be drunk with the drunkard. He will first draw you to be unclean in your thoughts, then to be unclean in your looks, then to be unclean in your words, and at last to be unclean in your practices. He will first draw you to look on the golden wedge, then to like the golden wedge, then to handle the golden wedge, and then at last to gain the golden wedge by wicked ways, though you run the risk of losing God and your soul#footnote[An Italian, having found his enemy at a disadvantage, promised him that if he would deny his faith, he would save his life. To save his life he denied his faith; and when he had done so, the Italian stabbed him, rejoicing that by this he had taken revenge on both soul and body at one time.] forever, as you may see in Gehazi, Achan and Judas, and in many in these days of ours. Sin never stands still. Ps 1:1: first #emph[ungodly,] then #emph[sinners,] then #emph[scorners.] Here they go on from sin to sin until they come to the top of sin, namely, “to sit in the seat of scorners,” or as the Septuagint has it (τῶν λοιμῶν), to aspire to the honor of the chair of pestilence. Augustine, writing on John, tells the story of a certain man who held the opinion that the devil made the fly, and not God. Someone said to him, “If the devil made flies, then the devil made worms, and God did not make them, for they are living creatures as well as flies.” “True,” he said, “the devil did make worms.” “But,” said the other, “if the devil made worms, then he made birds, beasts and man.” He granted it all. So, says Augustine, by denying God in the fly he came to deny God in man, and to deny the whole creation. By all this we see that yielding to lesser sins draws the soul to commit greater ones. Ah, how many in these days have fallen first to having low thoughts of #emph[Scripture] and #emph[ordinances,] then to slighting #emph[Scripture] and #emph[ordinances,] then to making a nose of wax of #emph[Scripture] and #emph[ordinances,] then to casting off #emph[Scripture] and #emph[ordinances,] and then at last to advancing and lifting up themselves and their Christ-dishonoring and soul-damning opinions#footnote[A young man was long tempted to kill his father, or to lie with his mother, or to be drunk. He thought he would yield to the lesser, namely, to be drunk, so that he might be rid of the greater. But when he was drunk, he both killed his father and lay with his mother.] above #emph[Scripture] and #emph[ordinances!] Sin gains on men’s souls by imperceptible degrees. Eccl 10:13: “The beginning of his talk is folly, and the end of his speech is evil madness.” Corruption in the heart, when it breaks out, is like a breach in a sea wall, which begins as a narrow passage until it eats through and casts down everything before it. The debates of the soul are quick and soon ended, and something may be done in a moment that may undo a man forever. When a man has begun to sin, he does not know where or when or how he will put a stop to sin. Usually the soul goes on from evil to evil, from folly to folly, until it is ripe for eternal misery. Men usually grow from being wicked to being very wicked, and from very wicked to utterly wicked, and then God counts them as nothing forever.

The third #emph[remedy] against this third device that Satan has to draw the soul to sin is to consider solemnly that it is sad to fall out with God over a trifle. The rich man would not give a crumb; therefore#footnote[Luke 16:21.] he was not to receive a drop. It is the greatest folly in the world to risk going to hell for a small matter. “I only tasted a little honey,” said Jonathan,#footnote[1 Sam 14:43.] “and now I must die.” It is a most unkind and unfaithful thing to break with God for a little. Little sins carry with them only little temptations to sin,#footnote[It was a vexation to King Lysimachus that his stopping to drink one small draught of water lost him his kingdom. And so it will vex some souls eternally at last that for some one little sin (compared with great transgressions) they have lost God, heaven and their souls forever.] and a man shows the most wickedness and unkindness when he sins on a little temptation. It is devilish to sin without a temptation; it is little less than devilish to sin on a little occasion. The less the temptation to sin is, the greater that sin is. Saul’s sin in not waiting for Samuel was not great in its matter, but it was great in its malice; for even if Samuel had not come at all, Saul should not have offered sacrifice. But this cost him dear: his soul and his kingdom. It is the greatest unkindness that can be shown to a friend to risk the complaining, bleeding and grieving of his soul on a light and slight occasion. So it is the greatest unkindness that can be shown to God, Christ and the Spirit for a soul to put God to complaining, Christ to bleeding and the Spirit to grieving by yielding to little sins. Therefore, when Satan says, “It is only a little one,” you must answer that often the greatest unkindness is shown to God’s glorious Majesty in acting the least folly, and therefore you will not displease your best and greatest friend by yielding to his least enemy.

The fourth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider seriously that there is great danger, indeed many times the most danger, in the smallest sins.#footnote[1 Cor 5:6.] “A little leaven works through the whole batch of dough.” If the serpent winds in his head, he will draw his whole body after it. Greater sins sooner startle the soul, and awaken and rouse the soul to repentance,#footnote[Caesar was stabbed with small daggers.] than lesser sins do. Little sins often slide into the soul, and breed and work secretly and unnoticed#footnote[Pope Adrian was choked by a gnat.] in the soul, until they become so strong as to trample on the soul and cut the throat of the soul. There is often the greatest danger to our bodies in the least diseases that hang on us, because we are apt to make#footnote[A scorpion is little, yet able to sting a lion to death; a mouse is only little, yet it kills an elephant if it gets up into its trunk.] light of them, and to neglect the timely use of means for removing them, until they have grown so strong that they prove fatal to us. So there is often the most danger in the least sins. We are apt to take no notice of them, and to neglect those heavenly helps by which they should be weakened and destroyed, until they have grown to such strength that we are ready to cry out, “The medicine is#footnote[The leopard, great as it is, is poisoned by a head of garlic.] too weak for the disease. I would pray, and I would hear the word, but I am afraid that sin has grown by degrees to such a head that I will never be able to prevail over it. As I have begun to fall,#footnote[The smallest errors often prove the most dangerous. It is as much treason to coin pennies as bigger pieces.] so I will utterly fall before it, and at last perish in it, unless the power and free grace of Christ act gloriously, beyond my present understanding and expectation.” The viper is killed by the little young ones that are nourished and cherished in her belly. So many men are eternally killed and betrayed by the little#footnote[One little misstep, in the eyes of the world, overshadows all a Christian’s graces, as one little cloud sometimes overshadows the whole body of the sun.] sins (as they call them) that are nourished in their own bosoms. “I do not know,” says one, “whether maintaining the least sin is not worse than committing the greatest; for the greatest may come from frailty, while maintaining the least shows obstinacy.” A little hole in the ship sinks it; a small breach in a sea wall carries away everything before it; a little stab at the heart kills a man; and a little sin, without a great deal of mercy, will damn a man.

The fifth remedy against this device#footnote[#emph[Melius mori fame quam Idolothytis vesci,] Augustine. It is better to die of hunger than to eat what is offered to idols.] of Satan is to consider solemnly that other saints have chosen to suffer the worst torments rather than commit the least sin, that is, the least as the world counts it. So you may see in Daniel and his companions, who would rather choose to burn and be cast to the lions than bow to the image that Nebuchadnezzar had set up. When this #emph[peccadillo] (in the world’s account) and a hot, fiery furnace stood in competition, so that they must either fall into sin or be cast into the#footnote[Many heathens would rather die than deceive or cheat one another, so faithful were they to one another. Will not these rise in judgment against many professing Christians in these days, who think nothing of taking advantage of one another?] fiery furnace, such was their tender regard for the honor and glory of God, and their hatred of and indignation against sin, that they would rather burn than sin. They knew that it was far better to burn for not sinning than for God and conscience to raise a hell, a fire, in their bosoms for sin. I have read of that noble servant of God, Marcus Arethusius, minister of a church in the time of Constantine, who in Constantine’s time had been the cause of overthrowing an idol’s temple. Afterward, when Julian came to be emperor, he would force the people of that place to build it up again. They were ready to do it, but Arethusius refused. At this, those who were his own people, to whom he preached, took him and stripped him of all his clothes, and abused his naked body, and gave it up to the children to cut with their penknives. Then they had him put in a basket, and anointed his naked body with honey, and set him in the sun to be stung by wasps. And they showed all this cruelty because he would not do anything toward building up this idol-temple. Indeed, they came to this: if he would do even the least toward it, if he would give only a halfpenny to it, they would save him. But he refused it all, though giving a halfpenny might have saved his life. And in doing this he only lived up to the principle that most Christians talk of, and all profess, but few come up to, namely, that we must choose to suffer the worst torments that men and devils can invent and inflict, rather than commit the least sin by which God would be dishonored, our consciences wounded, religion reproached and our own souls endangered.

The sixth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider seriously that the soul is never able to stand under the guilt and weight of the least sin when God brings it home to the soul. The least sin will press down and sink the boldest sinner as low as hell, when God opens the eyes of a sinner and makes him see the horrid filthiness and abominable vileness that is in sin. What creatures are so little, base and vile as lice or gnats? And yet by these little, poor creatures God so plagued stubborn Pharaoh and all Egypt that, fainting under it, they were forced to cry out, “This is the finger of God.” When little#footnote[Exod 8:16, 17, 18, 19.] creatures, indeed the least creatures, are armed with power from God, they will press down and sink the greatest, proudest and most stubborn tyrants that breathe. So when God puts a sword#footnote[The tyrant Maximinus, who had set out his proclamation engraved in brass for the utter abolishing of Christ and his religion, was eaten by lice.] into the hand of a little sin and arms it against the soul, the soul will faint and fall under it. Some who have only planned adultery, without ever actually committing it; others who found a trifle and did not make it a matter of conscience to return it, knowing by the light of natural conscience that they did not do as they would be done by; and others who have had some unworthy thought of God, have been so frightened, dismayed#footnote[#emph[Una guttula malae conscientiae totum mare mundani gaudii absorbet.] That is, one drop of an evil conscience swallows up the whole sea of worldly joy. “How great a pain, not to be borne, comes from the prick of this small thorn!” said one.] and terrified by these sins, which are small (in men’s account), that they have wished they had never been born; that they could take no delight in any earthly comfort; that they have been put at their wits’ end, ready to take their own lives, wishing themselves annihilated.

Mr. Perkins mentions a good man, but very poor, who, being ready to starve, stole a lamb. When he was about to eat it with his poor children, and (as his custom was before meals) to ask a blessing, he dared not do it, but fell into a great perplexity of #emph[conscience,] acknowledged his fault to the owner, and promised payment if ever he should be able.

The seventh remedy against this device is to consider solemnly that there is more evil in the least sin than in the greatest affliction. This appears as clear as the sun by the severe dealing of God the Father with his beloved Son, on whom he poured out all the vials of his fiercest wrath, and that for the least sin as well as for the greatest. “The wages#footnote[Death is the pay of the least sin; the best wages that the least sin gives its soldiers is death of every kind. In a strict sense there is no little sin, because there is no little God to sin against.] of sin is death”: that is, of sin without distinction, whether great or small. Oh, how this should make us tremble at the least spark of lust as much as at hell itself, considering that God the Father would not spare the Son of his bosom, no, not for the least sin, but would make him drink the dregs of his wrath! And so much for the remedies that may fence and preserve our souls from being drawn to sin by this third device of Satan.
